WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.648
Trace a cache with room for two exhibit descriptions
through accesses A, B, A, C, B.

00:00:05.648 --> 00:00:08.472
A and B initially miss and fill the cache.

00:00:08.472 --> 00:00:12.709
The second A is a hit and makes A the most recently
used entry.

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:16.878
C then misses and evicts B under least recently used
eviction.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.078
The final B misses and evicts A.

00:00:19.078 --> 00:00:25.611
Only one of these five accesses hits, showing that a
cache’s benefit depends on access order and

00:00:25.611 --> 00:00:28.198
capacity, not merely on its existence.

00:00:28.198 --> 00:00:34.731
In a managed cache design, application code performs
this lookup and fill around storage access.

00:00:34.731 --> 00:00:41.333
With a caching proxy, the client uses its normal
request interface while the intermediary manages

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:42.354
eligible reuse.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.511
Both still need a valid key and freshness policy.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:51.860
A load-balancing proxy performs a different
decision: which backend should receive work that
must be

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:52.495
forwarded?

00:00:52.495 --> 00:00:58.654
One component may implement both roles, but a
diagram should make clear whether it is selecting a

00:00:58.654 --> 00:01:01.511
worker, serving saved content, or doing both.

00:01:01.511 --> 00:01:08.105
Suppose an exhibit launch receives 800 eligible
reads each second and the measured cache hit ratio

00:01:08.105 --> 00:01:09.047
is 85 percent.

00:01:09.047 --> 00:01:15.708
Under a simple model with one backing read per miss
and no revalidation traffic, only 120 reads per

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.928
second reach the backing service.

00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:21.965
This gain follows from repeated demand for reusable
content.

00:01:22.011 --> 00:01:28.052
If requests become personalized or spread across
many rarely repeated items, the same cache may

00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:30.023
achieve a much lower hit ratio.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.121
Freshness is a separate decision.

00:01:32.121 --> 00:01:38.480
A public exhibit description might tolerate a short
stale interval, while a reservation confirmation

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.069
needs a different policy.

00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:46.046
Versioned image names let old and new images coexist
without changing an existing cache entry.

00:01:46.046 --> 00:01:51.477
For mutable responses, distinguish expiration,
validation and storage prohibition.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:57.571
No-cache can allow storage but requires successful
validation before reuse; no-store forbids

00:01:57.571 --> 00:01:58.101
storage.

00:01:58.101 --> 00:02:04.260
A private response cannot become a shared-cache
answer simply because it has a long freshness

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:04.856
lifetime.

00:02:04.856 --> 00:02:08.830
Always explain whose requests are allowed to reuse
an entry.
